<Base tier="lab" title="Reviewing a genus · Barcode Reliability Explorer" description="How invited reviewers answer genus questions in the Barcode Reliability Explorer: signing in, answering, how answers become decisions, and the question types.">
  <section class="station-hero station-hero-solo"><div class="station-hero-copy">
    <h1>Reviewing a genus</h1>
    <p class="lead">The Explorer gathers a genus’s ITS records from GenBank and iNaturalist and reports where the reference data cannot be trusted as it stands. Some of that needs a mycologist’s judgement: which sequence should fix what a name means, which names ITS cannot separate, which names have been applied to the wrong species. Those are the questions you are asked to answer.</p>
    <div class="tag-row"><span class="tag">for invited reviewers</span><span class="tag neutral">about 10 minutes to read</span></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section soft"><div class="container narrow xp" style="padding:0">
    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sign in</strong> at <a href="/explorer/decisions/">mycoview.com/explorer/decisions</a> with the e-mail address your invitation was sent to. You receive a one-time code by e-mail; there is no password.</li>
      <li><strong>Your first answers are test answers.</strong> They are saved and shown, but never count toward a decision, so you can try the pages freely. Once the site owner enables formal answers for you, you choose test or formal for each answer.</li>
      <li><strong>You answer blind.</strong> You see other reviewers’ answers to a question only after you have saved your own.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing here changes GenBank or iNaturalist.</strong> An answer is a curation decision with your name and reasoning attached. It changes how the Explorer treats records; the original records keep their deposited names.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="container narrow xp" style="padding:0">
    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <p>Open <strong>Genus decisions</strong> and choose a genus in <strong>Report</strong>. The list opens on the <strong>first queue</strong>: the few questions whose answers could change a species’ barcode gap or set a reference sequence. Each says why it is first. Choose <em>all</em> in <strong>Queue</strong> to see the rest. Use <strong>Your answer → not answered yet</strong> to see what is left for you.</p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:36px">Answering a question</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Read the question</strong> and the line under it: how many records it affects and whether it needs one reviewer or two.</li>
      <li><strong>Look at “Evidence from the report”.</strong> Distances are percentages of compared positions, and “Compared on” says which part of ITS was compared. A short region (ITS2 alone, say) carries less information than full ITS.</li>
      <li><strong>Check “The records”.</strong> Each accession opens its GenBank or iNaturalist record in a new tab. Names are shown as deposited: treat them as claims, not facts.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose an answer.</strong> Some answers ask for a detail, such as an accession, a name or a citation; give it in the box that appears.</li>
      <li><strong>Write your reasoning.</strong> It is required: what you compared and why it settles the question. Add <strong>citations</strong> where you can: DOIs, accessions, herbarium numbers, Index Fungorum or MycoBank numbers.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose test or formal</strong> and <strong>Save answer</strong>. “Next question you haven’t answered” takes you on, first-queue questions before the rest.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="muted small">Changed your mind? Answer again. The new answer replaces your earlier one, and both stay in the history.</p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:36px">How answers become decisions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Questions that change which name records belong to</strong> (D3, D4, D5, D8, D9, D10) need <strong>two reviewers giving the same formal answer</strong>. With one formal answer the question shows “awaiting second reviewer”.</li>
      <li><strong>The others</strong> (D1, D2, D6, D7) are decided by <strong>one formal answer</strong>.</li>
      <li>If formal answers differ, the question is <strong>disputed</strong>. Each reviewer can see the other’s reasoning and change their own answer; nothing is decided until they agree.</li>
      <li>A decided answer is applied to the genus’s working set straight away (for example, records are relabelled), with your name, the date and the reason. Answers that change the underlying data, such as a new reference sequence or an excluded record, take effect in the genus’s next rebuild.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section soft"><div class="container xp" style="padding:0">
    <h2>The kinds of question</h2>
    <div class="xp-panel xp-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>What it asks</th><th>Possible answers</th><th>Reviewers</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{types.map(([a, b, c, d]) => <tr><td><strong>{a}</strong></td><td>{b}</td><td class="muted">{c}</td><td>{d}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p class="muted small">Every question also offers “other”, which asks you to explain.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="container narrow xp" style="padding:0">
    <h2>Tools that help</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Working sets</strong> (in the Explorer’s menu): every record of the genus with its working name, its role (type reference, included, outgroup, excluded) and why it has that role. Search by accession or name, filter by role, and see every change made by a decision.</li>
      <li><strong>Place a sequence</strong> (a tab on the working-set page): paste one ITS sequence to see which records and names it sits nearest to, how far, and over how much of it. This is a placement, not an identification: no threshold is applied and no name is chosen. The sequence is compared in your browser and not sent anywhere.</li>
      <li><strong>Downloads</strong> on the working-set page: FASTA of the references, included and outgroup records, or of the type references only, and a CSV of every record, for your own alignments and trees.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:36px">Good habits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>“Unresolved”, “needs full ITS” and “needs more data” are good answers.</strong> Saying the evidence cannot decide is better than guessing, and it tells everyone what is missing.</li>
      <li><strong>Mind the region.</strong> Many type sequences are short ITS2 fragments of old herbarium material. Two names looking identical on ITS2 alone says little about full ITS.</li>
      <li><strong>Mind where an iNaturalist name came from.</strong> A record labelled “iNat · name from sequence?” may have been named from its own sequence, so its name is not independent evidence about that sequence.</li>
      <li><strong>Write reasoning someone else can check:</strong> which records, which distances, which paper. Your answer is read by the next reviewer and kept with the decision.</li>
      <li><strong>Unsure about a question, or think it is wrong?</strong> Answer “other”, say what is wrong, and tell the person who invited you.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:30px"><a class="button primary" href="/explorer/decisions/">Go to Genus decisions</a> <span class="muted small">Restricted to invited reviewers.</span> · <a href="/investigate/explorer/">About the Explorer</a></p>
  </div></section>
</Base>
